 {"t": "Lubrication System Fill and Prime", "g": "Startup", "tier": "compounded",
  "d": "The first start is when lubrication matters most: dry bearings and gears fail in minutes. This record covers filling and priming the lubrication system before first rotation: filling reservoirs with the specified lubricant, priming pumps, bleeding air, verifying flow to every point, and the pre-start lubrication checklist.\nThe procedure includes lubricant verification (right product, clean), filter installation, and the flow verification that proves oil reaches the bearings.",
  "h": "Fill and prime works because rotating parts need their oil film from the first revolution: there is no grace period for dry running. Priming the pump and bleeding air ensures the system delivers oil immediately instead of pumping air while bearings run dry. Verifying flow to every point catches the blocked line and the closed valve that starve one bearing while the rest look fine. Using the specified lubricant matters from the start because the system's clearances and the lubricant's viscosity were designed together. The pre-start checklist is the last gate: oil at level, filters in, flows verified, no leaks - then and only then does the machine turn.",
  "f": ["Fill with the specified lubricant; verify the product, not just the barrel label.",
        "Prime pumps and bleed air; bearings need oil from the first revolution.",
        "Verify flow to every lubrication point; one blocked line starves one bearing.",
        "Install filters before filling; filling through no filter invites contamination.",
        "Check for leaks at every joint before start.",
        "Complete the pre-start lubrication checklist; it is the gate to first rotation."],
  "r": ["Specified lubricant in sealed containers; verification of product.",
        "Filters installed; priming and bleeding tools.",
        "Flow verification method for each lubrication point.",
        "Spill containment for filling operations."],
  "s": [0, 6, 5], "c": [4, 5, 7],
  "demo": ("checklist", {"items": [("Correct lubricant filled", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Pump primed, air bled", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Flow verified all points", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("No leaks found", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0)]})},
 {"t": "First-Energization Procedure", "g": "Startup", "tier": "compounded",
  "d": "First energization is the moment installation meets reality. This record defines the controlled procedure: final pre-energization checks, energizing in stages (control power first, then main power), verifying voltages and phase rotation, and the watch period with defined abort criteria.\nThe procedure includes who must be present, the communication protocol, and the immediate de-energization criteria.",
  "h": "Staged energization works because it isolates faults to the stage just energized: control power first proves the control system and safety circuits; main power then proves the power circuits. If something is wrong, the fault appears in the current stage, not buried in a whole-machine energization. Pre-energization checks (torque, insulation, grounding, no foreign objects) catch the errors that energization would turn into damage. Defined abort criteria - smoke, unusual sound, wrong voltage, protection trip - remove hesitation: anyone who sees an abort condition calls it, and the procedure stops. The watch period after energization lets thermal and electrical stabilization reveal faults before load is applied.",
  "f": ["Complete all pre-energization checks before the first stage; no shortcuts.",
        "Energize in stages: control power first, then main power.",
        "Verify voltages and phase rotation at each stage.",
        "Define abort criteria in advance; anyone can call an abort.",
        "Keep non-essential personnel clear during energization.",
        "Hold a watch period after energization before applying load."],
  "r": ["Pre-energization checklist, fully signed off.",
        "Meters for voltage and phase verification at each stage.",
        "Communication protocol and abort authority defined.",
        "Fire precautions and first-aid readiness for electrical work."],
  "s": [2, 1, 5], "c": [0, 1, 4],
  "demo": ("steps", {"steps": [("Pre-energization checks signed off", 30), ("Energize control power, verify", 15), ("Verify safety circuits functional", 15), ("Energize main power in stages", 20), ("Verify voltages and rotation", 15), ("Watch period, monitor for faults", 30)]})},
 {"t": "Dry-Run and Rotation Checks", "g": "Startup", "tier": "compounded",
  "d": "Before the machine does real work, it must prove it can move correctly with no load. This record covers the dry run: verifying rotation direction, checking for interference through the full travel, exercising every axis and function, and listening and feeling for abnormalities.\nThe procedure includes the bump test for rotation direction, full-travel checks, and the criteria that must be met before load is applied.",
  "h": "Dry running works because no-load operation reveals mechanical and control faults at safe energy: interference, wrong rotation, binding, and control errors all appear without the forces of production load. The bump test (momentary energization) checks rotation direction before momentum builds - a pump run backward can unscrew its impeller, a fan run backward moves no air while looking fine. Full-travel checks prove clearances through the entire range, not just the mid position. Exercising every function proves the control logic end to end. Abnormal sound, heat, or vibration at no load is a stop condition: faults only get worse under load, never better.",
  "f": ["Bump-test rotation direction before any sustained run.",
        "Check full travel and clearances through the entire range of motion.",
        "Exercise every axis and function the machine has.",
        "Listen and feel: abnormal sound, heat, or vibration at no load is a stop.",
        "Verify lubrication flow during the dry run.",
        "Meet all dry-run criteria before applying any load."],
  "r": ["Dry-run procedure with the functions and travels to exercise.",
        "Rotation direction reference for every motor and axis.",
        "Vibration and temperature observation during the run.",
        "Authority to stop on any abnormality."],
  "s": [0, 6, 5], "c": [4, 7, 5],
  "demo": ("checklist", {"items": [("Rotation directions correct", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Full travel clear", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("All functions exercised", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("No abnormal sound/heat/vibration", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Lubrication flowing", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0)]})},
 {"t": "Control Loop Tuning at Startup", "g": "Startup", "tier": "compounded",
  "d": "New installations need their control loops tuned to the actual machine: the PID values that worked on the last machine are a starting point, not a result. This record covers startup tuning: the safe tuning sequence, step-response testing, setting proportional, integral, and derivative terms, and verifying stability across the operating range.\nThe procedure includes the safety limits during tuning, the bump test method, and documentation of final parameters.",
  "h": "Loop tuning works by matching the controller to the real process dynamics: every machine has different inertia, friction, and lags, so copied parameters are guesses. The step-response test reveals the process's true behavior - dead time, time constant, gain - from which proper tuning follows. Tuning in a safe sequence (start conservative, increase carefully) keeps the machine stable while learning its dynamics. Testing across the operating range matters because processes are nonlinear: a loop tuned at one speed can oscillate at another. Documented final parameters with the test data let the next engineer understand and maintain the tuning instead of starting over.",
  "f": ["Start from conservative tuning; increase carefully while watching stability.",
        "Use step-response tests to learn the real process dynamics.",
        "Set safety limits before tuning; an oscillating machine is a hazard.",
        "Verify stability across the full operating range, not one point.",
        "Test disturbance rejection, not just setpoint response.",
        "Document final parameters with the test data that justifies them."],
  "r": ["Tuning procedure with the safe sequence and limits.",
        "Trend recording for step-response analysis.",
        "Authority to stop on oscillation or instability.",
        "Parameter documentation with test evidence."],
  "s": [5, 0, 6], "c": [4, 6, 7],
  "demo": ("case", {"metrics": [("setpoint overshoot", 25.0, 4.0, "%", "down"), ("settling time", 45.0, 12.0, "s", "down")]})},
 {"t": "Load Testing Procedure", "g": "Acceptance Testing", "tier": "compounded",
  "d": "Load testing proves the machine does its rated work: applying load in controlled steps up to and including rated capacity, measuring performance at each step, and verifying temperatures, vibrations, and outputs stay within specification.\nThe procedure covers the load plan, measurement points, the step sequence with stabilization holds, and the pass criteria from the specification.",
  "h": "Load testing works by exercising the machine through its real duty: faults that hide at no load - weak power stages, marginal cooling, slipping drives - appear under load. Stepped loading with stabilization holds is the safety logic: each step is verified before the next, so a problem appears at partial load instead of full. Measuring at each step builds the performance curve that proves rated capacity, not just survival. Temperature and vibration trending during the test catch the developing fault while it is still a reading, not a failure. The specification's pass criteria keep the test honest: the machine must meet its rated numbers, not merely run.",
  "f": ["Load in controlled steps with stabilization holds; verify each step before the next.",
        "Measure performance at every step; build the curve that proves rated capacity.",
        "Trend temperature and vibration throughout; developing faults show as trends.",
        "Apply the specification's pass criteria, not invented ones.",
        "Have the rated load available and safely applicable before starting.",
        "Stop on any parameter exceeding its limit; investigate, do not push through."],
  "r": ["Load plan with steps, holds, and measurement points.",
        "Rated load safely applicable (test weights, process load, dynamometer).",
        "Calibrated instruments for all measured parameters.",
        "Specification pass criteria in writing before the test."],
  "s": [0, 5, 6], "c": [4, 7, 5],
  "demo": ("tolerance", {"nominal": 100.0, "tol": 5.0, "unit": "% rated output", "readings": [98.5, 101.2, 99.8, 100.5]})},
 {"t": "Performance Verification Runs", "g": "Acceptance Testing", "tier": "compounded",
  "d": "Beyond rated load, the machine must perform its actual duty: rate, quality, and consistency over time. This record covers performance verification runs: defining the acceptance criteria from the contract, running at production conditions, measuring output rate and quality, and the statistical basis for acceptance.\nThe procedure includes the run duration that proves consistency, sampling plans for quality, and handling of borderline results.",
  "h": "Performance verification works because contracts promise performance, not just operation: the machine must make rate at quality, consistently. Running at production conditions (real materials, real rates, real environment) is the only honest test - laboratory conditions flatter. Duration matters because consistency is proven over time; a machine that makes rate for ten minutes but drifts over hours fails the real requirement. Sampling plans with statistical criteria separate real performance from luck: a defined sample size and acceptance number make the verdict objective. Borderline results get extended runs, not arguments - more data resolves what debate cannot.",
  "f": ["Define acceptance criteria from the contract before the run.",
        "Run at real production conditions: materials, rates, environment.",
        "Run long enough to prove consistency, not just capability.",
        "Use a sampling plan with statistical criteria for quality verdicts.",
        "Resolve borderline results with extended runs, not debate.",
        "Record everything; the run data is the acceptance evidence."],
  "r": ["Contract performance criteria in writing.",
        "Production-representative materials and conditions.",
        "Sampling plan and measurement methods.",
        "Data recording for the full run duration."],
  "s": [0, 5, 6], "c": [6, 4, 7],
  "demo": ("case", {"metrics": [("output rate", 92.0, 101.0, "% of contract", "up"), ("defect rate", 3.2, 0.8, "%", "down")]})},
 {"t": "Punch List Management", "g": "Acceptance Testing", "tier": "compounded",
  "d": "No installation is perfect at first test; the punch list is the disciplined way to finish it. This record covers punch list management: capturing every deficiency found during testing, assigning responsibility and dates, verifying each correction, and closing items only on evidence.\nThe procedure includes the item format (location, description, criterion, evidence of closure), the review rhythm, and the rule that acceptance waits for the list - or for written agreement on deferred items.",
  "h": "Punch list management works because it converts vague dissatisfaction into specific, assigned, dated work: each item names the deficiency, the location, the acceptance criterion, and who fixes it by when. The closure-on-evidence rule prevents the common failure of items marked complete without verification - an item closes when the test proves it, not when someone says it is done. The review rhythm (regular walkthroughs) keeps the list moving instead of stagnating. Written agreement on deferred items protects both sides: the owner knows what is outstanding and when it will be done, and the contractor is not held to an infinite list. Acceptance gated on the list ensures the machine is truly finished.",
  "f": ["Capture every deficiency specifically: location, description, criterion.",
        "Assign each item: responsible party and due date.",
        "Close items only on evidence - retest, do not take word for it.",
        "Review the list on a rhythm; stagnant lists grow.",
        "Defer items only by written agreement with dates.",
        "Gate acceptance on the list; the machine is finished when the list says so."],
  "r": ["Punch list form with the required fields.",
        "Test criteria for verifying each correction.",
        "Review schedule with owner and contractor.",
        "Authority to hold acceptance on open items."],
  "s": [0, 6, 7], "c": [6, 4, 7],
  "demo": ("steps", {"steps": [("Capture deficiencies during testing", 45), ("Assign responsibility and dates", 20), ("Execute corrections", 120), ("Verify each correction by test", 60), ("Close on evidence, review rhythm", 30)]})},
 {"t": "Operator Training Handover", "g": "Acceptance Testing", "tier": "compounded",
  "d": "A machine is only commissioned when its operators can run it safely and well. This record covers the training handover: the curriculum (normal operation, abnormal response, routine care, emergency actions), hands-on practice under supervision, competency verification, and the training records.\nThe procedure includes tailoring to the operators' experience, the supervised operation period, and the documentation package the operators receive.",
  "h": "Training handover works because equipment knowledge must transfer from the installer to the daily operators: the people who run the machine determine its life and safety. Hands-on practice under supervision builds real competence; classroom alone produces operators who have heard about the machine but never felt it. Competency verification - each operator demonstrating the key tasks - proves the training worked instead of assuming it. The emergency actions get special emphasis because emergencies are when training matters most and when untrained operators cause the most harm. Training records prove the handover happened, which matters for safety accountability and for onboarding future operators.",
  "f": ["Train on the actual machine, hands-on, not just in a classroom.",
        "Cover normal operation, abnormal response, routine care, and emergency actions.",
        "Verify competency: each operator demonstrates the key tasks.",
        "Emphasize emergency actions; emergencies are when training matters most.",
        "Provide the operator documentation package: manuals, quick guides, contacts.",
        "Record the training: who, what, when, and the competency results."],
  "r": ["Training curriculum matched to the equipment and the operators.",
        "Supervised hands-on time on the commissioned machine.",
        "Competency checklist for verification.",
        "Operator documentation package ready at handover."],
  "s": [0, 4, 6], "c": [6, 4, 7],
  "demo": ("checklist", {"items": [("Normal operation demonstrated", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Abnormal response known", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Emergency actions verified", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Routine care understood", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0), ("Training recorded", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0)]})},
